<Base title="A valley of voices" description="Language, poetry, local knowledge and making in Swat, through named community and research sources." section="culture" image="bahrain-town">
<header class="page-heading"><span class="eyebrow">Living culture · Language & belonging</span><h1>A valley<br/>of voices.</h1><p class="deck">A place is also the words used to know it. The songs people carry. The things made by hand.</p></header><div class="voice-display" aria-hidden="true"><span>Pashto</span><span>Torwali</span><span>Gawri</span></div>
<div class="reading-layout"><aside class="reading-aside"><span class="eyebrow">People make culture</span><p>Three starting points.<br/>No language boundary fits<br/>inside a single map marker.</p><a class="underlink" href="/atlas/?lens=language">Locate these connections ↗</a></aside><article class="reading-copy"><div class="language-tabs" aria-label="Language stories" data-story-tabs hidden><button id="tab-torwali" data-panel="torwali">Torwali</button><button id="tab-gawri" data-panel="gawri">Gawri</button><button id="tab-pashto" data-panel="pashto">Pashto & plurality</button></div><section class="language-panel" id="torwali"><span class="eyebrow">Bahrain & surrounding communities</span><h2>Poetry belongs<br/>to the present.</h2><p>Torwali is carried through everyday speech, poetry and song. In Bahrain and surrounding communities, cultural work connects that inheritance to education and new forms of expression.</p><p>Educator and organiser Zubair Torwali describes recording songs for <em>Manjoora</em> and bringing people together through the Simam festival. His account gives these efforts names, people and purpose.</p><p>Language renewal involves choices: what to record, how to teach, and how a younger generation can take part. Follow his account to encounter the music and words in their own context.</p><References ids={['torwali','culture']}/><ArrowLink href="/places/bahrain/" label="Bahrain: river & language"/></section><section class="language-panel" id="gawri"><span class="eyebrow">Kalam · Ushu · Utror</span><h2>A language crosses<br/>the ridgeline.</h2><p>Gawri, also called Kalami, is spoken in upper Swat and in communities beyond it in Dir Kohistan. The geographical reach of a language does not stop at the edge of a valley.</p><p>Joan Baart and Muhammad Zaman Sagar’s introduction connects the language with Kalam, Ushu and Utror. It also explains relationships among speech communities across the mountains.</p><p>Names can appear with different spellings in different sources: Ushu and Usho, Utror and Utrot. These variations are a reminder that writing a place name involves translating between scripts and conventions.</p><References ids={['gawriField']}/><ArrowLink href="/places/kalam/" label="Kalam: the upper branches"/></section><section class="language-panel" id="pashto"><span class="eyebrow">A wider linguistic landscape</span><h2>More than<br/>a single voice.</h2><p>Pashto is widely spoken in Swat. Gawri and Torwali add distinct linguistic histories to the upper and middle valleys. Movement, education and work also bring languages into contact.</p><p>This introduction does not assign one identity to everyone in a town. Community membership and language use are more varied than a coloured region on a map can show.</p><p>The atlas’s language lens therefore offers documented connections rather than boundaries. It keeps the places visible while changing the questions we ask of them.</p><References ids={['gawriField','torwali']}/><ArrowLink href="/atlas/?lens=language" label="Explore the language lens"/></section></article></div>
<div class="photo-band captioned"><Photo id="bahrain-town" caption sizes="100vw"/></div>
<section class="craft-strip"><Photo id="swat-museum" caption/><div><span class="eyebrow">Making · Memory · Use</span><h2>Knowledge<br/>held in things.</h2><p>Swat Museum’s ethnographic collections include carved wood, embroidery and jewellery. The South Asia Collection’s exhibition on Swat wood carving offers another route into objects, motifs and the work of making.</p><p>These collections are starting points for looking, not substitutes for a maker’s own voice. We link to documented objects and accounts rather than inventing an artisan’s story.</p><References ids={['museum','craft']}/></div></section><div class="culture-note">A landscape can be known plant by plant.<span>Research in Madyan and neighbouring villages records local plant names and reported uses. <a class="underlink" href="/places/madyan/">Read the Madyan note ↗</a></span></div>
